\title{Grid Quantization of Threshold Measurements:\\ Rounding, Resolution, and the Arithmetic of Sweep Design}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2 September 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop the arithmetic theory of measuring the crossing point (``knee'') of a monotone
profile by sampling it on a restricted set of budgets. Let $f:\NN\to\RR$ be nondecreasing,
let $\tau$ be a gate met somewhere, and let $\kstar=\min\{k: f(k)\ge\tau\}$ be the true knee.
For an unbounded \emph{sweep grid} $G\subseteq\NN$ define the \emph{reading}
$\rd_G(k)=\min\{g\in G: g\ge k\}$. Our first result, the Measurement Theorem, states that the
knee obtained by sweeping only over $G$ equals $\rd_G(\kstar)$ exactly: a grid measurement is
never an approximation with an error term, it is a deterministic rounding of the truth. We show
$\rd_G$ is a closure operator on $\NN$ with fixed-point set $G$, and prove a uniqueness theorem:
any inflationary, monotone, idempotent read-out whose fixed points are the grid \emph{is} $\rd_G$.
Specialising, the doubling grid reads $k$ as $2^{\lceil\log_2 k\rceil}$ and is exact precisely
when the base-two digit sum of $k$ equals $1$; the step-$d$ arithmetic grid is exact precisely
when $d\mid k$. We apply the theory to a five-point measurement table for a top-$k$
attention-budget profile at context length $1024$ with gate $0.98$, proving that every
nondecreasing profile matching the table yields sweep reading $20$, that the same hypotheses
force only $12<\kstar\le 20$, and that this bracket is tight. Since the swept set omits $16$,
the values $\kstar=16$ and $\kstar=20$ are indistinguishable from the data: the deployment claim
``$20$ keys suffice, $12$ do not'' is certified, while the claim that the cross-context chain
$16<20<24$ is strictly increasing is not. Finally we quantify sweep design: a step-$d$ sweep
resolves $\lfloor N/d\rfloor$ of the budgets in $(0,N]$, a doubling sweep at most $\log_2 N+1$ and
can emit at most $\lceil\log_2 N\rceil+1$ distinct verdicts; the arithmetic sweeps resolving $k$
are exactly its divisors, so resolution power is the divisor function $\tau$; and a step-$d$ sweep
resolves a finite chain $K$ iff $d\mid\gcd K$, making step $4=\gcd\{16,20,24\}$ the coarsest
adequate sweep. A bridge result shows the misreadings $20\mapsto 32$ and $112\mapsto 128$ are
instances of one theorem about binary staircase numbers $2^b(2^j-1)$.
\end{abstract}

